\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{Prerequisites and conventions}
\date{2026-11-09}


\p{The notes assume a background in mathematical physics rather than develop one.
The citations here are for readers who want to fill in this background.
Differential forms, with the potential a #{1}-form, the field strength its exterior
derivative, and the sourced equations needing a Hodge star, are recalled in
\ref{fdip-00AA} and used in \ref{fdip-00AB} and \ref{fdip-00AD}; Hassani treats
the exterior calculus in \citet{Section 28.5, p. 888}{hassani2013mathematical},
and Szekeres treats the exterior algebra, electrodynamics, and forms on manifolds
in \citet{Chapter 8, p. 204}{szekeres2004course},
\citet{Section 9.4, p. 244}{szekeres2004course},
\citet{Chapter 16, p. 447}{szekeres2004course} and
\citet{Chapter 17, p. 481}{szekeres2004course}. Clifford algebras
of a quadratic space, their even parts and the spinors they act on, are used from
Part V on, with the square-quotient convention stated in \ref{fdip-000P}; Hassani
develops the algebras in \citet{Chapter 27, p. 829}{hassani2013mathematical} and
their representations in \citet{Chapter 31, p. 987}{hassani2013mathematical}, and
Szekeres treats quaternions and Clifford algebras in
\citet{Section 6.3, p. 157}{szekeres2004course}. Gauge theory is assumed at the level of
connections, gauge transformations and the actions invariant under them, as in
Hassani's chapters on fiber bundles
\citet{Chapter 34, p. 1079}{hassani2013mathematical} and gauge theories
\citet{Chapter 35, p. 1099}{hassani2013mathematical}. Group theory
and its representation theory are assumed as Hassani supplies the groups in
\citet{Chapter 23, p. 701}{hassani2013mathematical} and the representations in
\citet{Chapter 24, p. 725}{hassani2013mathematical}, and as Szekeres supplies them in
\citet{Chapter 2, p. 27}{szekeres2004course},
\citet{Section 4.4, p. 120}{szekeres2004course} and
\citet{Section 5.3, p. 141}{szekeres2004course}; the finite-group representation
theory that Part IV uses, characters and isotypic components, is developed there.
The Euclidean formulation of quantum field theory is assumed as well: a law given by a
measure, a Hilbert space of states recovered by reflection positivity
\ref{fdip-007L}, in the sense of
\citet{Definition 10.5, p. 441}{friedli2017statistical}, and fermions carried by
anticommuting variables, as in
\citet{Section 9.1, p. 19}{wingate2025lattice}. Geometric calculus is
not required, but a reader who knows it may recognize in these constructions the
unifications it presents, a single derivative acting through the geometric product and
fields of different grades in one algebra, the perspective of
\citek{hestenes2012clifford}, of \citet{Section 7.1}{doran2003geometric} and of
\citet{pp. 63-64}{macdonald2012vector}.}

\p{On a finite periodic lattice of #{L} sites with spacing #{a}, the momenta
are #{2\pi n/(La)}, a finite set in the Brillouin zone running from
#{-\pi /a}, excluded, to #{\pi /a}, included. The finite-site difference
operators have bounded spectra; a finite time lattice also cuts off
frequency, with a spacing of its own. This spatial momentum cutoff does
not bound the energy of a scalar field with real-valued coordinates:
for a nonempty finite spatial graph, the interacting Hamiltonian in
\ref{fdip-00H3} has an unbounded discrete spectrum. A probability law is
a measure on the values the fields take. The measure theory assumed is measure
spaces, the Lebesgue integral, the monotone and dominated convergence theorems
\citet{Chapter 12, p. 96}{schilling2017measures}, product measures and Fubini's
theorem \citet{Chapter 14, p. 136}{schilling2017measures}, and the linear change
of variables \citet{Chapter 16, p. 164}{schilling2017measures}, together with the
#{L^p} spaces of \citet{Chapter 13, p. 116}{schilling2017measures}. Szekeres
gives a short account of measure and integration in
\citet{Chapter 11, Sections 11.1-11.3, p. 287}{szekeres2004course}. The proofs
cite Tao's preliminary version \citek{tao2011measure}; that book is not required
reading beside these. At a finite cutoff the carrier is finite, but the
real numbers return at the law, since a nondegenerate, positive-definite
Gaussian law on real
coordinates has a density rather than a finite table of weights
\citet{Appendix A.2, equation (A.4), p. 200}{rasmussen2006gaussian},
\citet{Proposition B.55, p. 505}{friedli2017statistical}. A nonnegative-definite
Gaussian law, including a singular one, factors as in
\citet{Lemma B.54, p. 504}{friedli2017statistical}. Comparing these laws is an
integration, and a retained precision is a Schur complement \ref{fdip-0020}.
Conditioning and marginalization belong to that comparison. A reader should be
able to complete a square and to integrate an exponential of a quadratic form.}

\p{The algebra is that of a commutative scalar ring with identity, its modules and its
algebras. A \vocab{ring} is a set with addition and multiplication: addition
makes it an abelian group, multiplication makes it a monoid, and multiplication
distributes over addition. The scalar ring #{R} is commutative and has an identity
#{1_R}. An #{R}-module is an abelian group #{M} with a scalar multiplication
#{R\times M\longrightarrow M} that is additive in each argument, respects
multiplication in #{R}, and sends #{1_R} to the identity map. An
#{R}-\vocab{algebra} is an associative unital ring #{A} with a unital
\vocab{ring homomorphism} #{\iota:R\longrightarrow A} whose image lies in the
center of #{A}. Multiplication in #{A} may be noncommutative. The scalar
multiplication is #{r\cdot x=\iota(r)x}. Those axiom lists are the algebra suite's
\vocabk{ring}{ca-000U}, \vocabk{module}{ca-000X}, \vocabk{algebra}{ca-0016} and
\vocabk{ring homomorphism}{ca-0014}, and a tree that uses a further structure links it
where it uses it.}
